title: One switch, two lights
mode: draw
chapter: Signals and gates
check: behaves
---- intro
A wire can split: one output can feed as many inputs as you like, and every one of them gets the same value. Engineers call this **fan-out**.

Here there is one switch `a` and two lights, `y1` and `y2`, with nothing wired yet.

You make a wire by **dragging from the small square on the right of a part** (its output) **to the small square on the left of another** (its input). To feed a second light, drag from the same output again.
---- task
Wire the switch `a` to both lights, so they both follow it.
---- start
input a 0 40
led y1 260 0
led y2 260 90
---- reference
input a
led y1, y2
y1 = a
y2 = a
---- answer
input a 0 40
led y1 260 0
led y2 260 90
a -> y1
a -> y2
---- hint1
Start at the small square on the right edge of the switch, hold the mouse down, and let go on the square on the left of `y1`.
---- hint2
Now do it again from the same square on the switch, this time to `y2`.
---- solution
Two wires, both starting at `a`: one to `y1`, one to `y2`. Click `a` and both lights follow it.
---- done
One output, two inputs. Inside a real chip, one gate's output often feeds dozens of others.
